.Dd July 1, 2026
.Dt XTC_PREEMPT 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_preempt_arm ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_disarm ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_supported ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_ticks ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_tick_pending
.Nd per-worker preemption timer seam
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_preempt.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_arm "int64_t interval_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_disarm "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_supported "void"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn xtc_preempt_ticks "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_tick_pending "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions manage a per-thread preemption timer -- the seam the
libxtc preemption facility is built on (see the preemption design in the
project docs).  On most platforms (Linux, the BSDs, illumos, AIX) the
timer measures the calling thread's own CPU time, so a busy fiber
accrues ticks while an idle worker does not; on macOS the tick source is
instead wall-clock (see the macOS paragraph below for why and the
consequence).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_arm
arms a timer on the calling thread that fires every
.Fa interval_ns
of the underlying tick source (that thread's CPU time on most
platforms; wall-clock time on macOS), delivering an internal signal to
the thread.  Re-arming re-sets the interval.  It returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a non-positive interval, or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
where no tick source is available.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_disarm
stops and deletes the calling thread's timer; it is safe to call when no
timer is armed.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_supported
returns non-zero where the platform provides a tick source --
per-thread CPU-time timers on Linux, the BSDs, illumos, and AIX, or a
kqueue
.Dv EVFILT_TIMER
on macOS (see below) -- and zero otherwise, in which case
.Fn xtc_preempt_arm
returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_ticks
returns the total number of timer ticks the calling thread has observed
since arming -- telemetry, and the metric that confirms the seam works.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_tick_pending
returns non-zero if a tick has fired and is unconsumed, clearing the
flag.  The cooperative-assisted preemption path consults it at safe
points to decide whether to yield.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Ft int
functions return
.Dv XTC_OK
on success or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code as described above.
.Sh NOTES
The timer only records ticks; it does not by itself preempt a running
fiber.  Higher layers of the preemption facility act on the ticks --
cooperatively (a yield at the next safe point) or, when the involuntary
path is enabled, via a signal-context involuntary yield.  Nothing arms
the timer unless a caller opts in, so the cooperative fast path is
unchanged when unused.
.Pp
The involuntary path, which yields a fiber at the interrupted
instruction, is EXPERIMENTAL, off by default, and documented in
.Xr xtc_preempt_involuntary 3
(its own header,
.In xtc_preempt_involuntary.h ) .
A program that links libxtc or libc statically must mark the code it
wants preemptible
.Pq Dv XTC_PREEMPTIBLE
or enabling it fails.
.Pp
Signal masks: the ucontext substrate restores each fiber's saved signal
mask on every switch; the fcontext substrate does not touch the mask,
so a fiber runs with the mask of the thread it is on.  On an
.Xr xtc_exec 3
worker that is every signal blocked except the synchronous faults
.Pq Dv SIGSEGV , SIGBUS , SIGFPE , SIGILL ,
.Dv SIGABRT
and the preemption signal, the same set a ucontext fiber unblocks.  A
fiber on a loop the application runs on its own thread
.Pq Xr xtc_loop_run 3
runs with that thread's mask under fcontext, so a process-directed
signal that thread leaves unblocked can be handled on the fiber's
stack; ucontext blocked those inside fibers.  A fiber that changes its
thread's mask with
.Xr pthread_sigmask 3
changes it for every fiber that later runs on that thread under
fcontext; restore it before yielding.
.Pp
On macOS the involuntary path (Phase 2, the signal-context redirect)
remains Linux-only: it needs the fiber's interrupted machine state
rewritten via an on-stack trampoline
(see
.Sx SEE ALSO ) ,
and that redirect is gated inside the active coroutine substrate, which
declines on
.Dv __APPLE__ .
The Phase 1 cooperative-assisted tick source, however, DOES now work on
macOS: since there is no per-thread
.Fn timer_create
/
.Dv CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID
(process-wide
.Fn setitimer
is unsuitable for per-thread accounting), macOS instead drives
.Fn xtc_preempt_arm
with a kqueue
.Dv EVFILT_TIMER
on a small private per-thread kqueue: a dedicated helper thread blocks
in
.Fn kevent
and, on every timer fire, delivers the SAME internal signal a
POSIX-timer platform's kernel would, driving the identical tick
counter and pending flag this page documents above.  The one real,
user-visible difference: kqueue has no CPU-time filter, so the macOS
tick source is WALL-CLOCK, not CPU-time -- an idle macOS worker still
ticks, where an idle Linux/BSD worker does not.  Net effect: on macOS
.Nm
correctly drives Phase 1 cooperative-assisted preemption (a fiber that
calls a yield point periodically IS time-sliced), but still degrades to
the cooperative path only for Phase 2 -- a fiber that never reaches a
yield point will not be involuntarily interrupted on macOS.  This
Phase-2 gap is a real limitation, not merely an untested corner; do not
rely on involuntary fairness for uncooperative CPU-bound work on macOS.
The Phase 1 kqueue tick source runs on Apple Silicon in CI on every
push (test_preempt /macos_kqueue_timer, test_preempt_p1 /timeslice).
.Pp
On the io_uring backend the executor drives the cooperative (Phase 1)
tick source from the ring itself rather than from this per-thread
signal timer: it arms a rearmed
.Dv IORING_OP_TIMEOUT
SQE on a small dedicated ring, and the
.Dq preempt is due
check becomes two relaxed/acquire loads of that ring's completion
head/tail -- no signal, no syscall, and none of the wrong-thread
signal-delivery hazard the CPU-time timer historically fought.  A long
compute fiber that reaches a yield point is therefore time-sliced with
NO signal delivered on that backend (measured at roughly a third the
per-check cost of the signal-flag path).  The signal timer this page
documents remains the portable Phase 1 source on epoll, kqueue, IOCP,
select, and the other readiness backends, where there is no ring to
read; and it is still armed on io_uring too whenever the involuntary
(Phase 2) path is enabled, since redirecting a fiber that never reaches
a yield point requires the signal handler.  This ring-pointer preempt
mechanism is INSPIRED BY Glommio (Glauber Costa / ScyllaDB): its
.Fn need_preempt
(glommio
.Pa src/reactor.rs
and
.Pa src/sys/uring.rs
preempt_pointers) is likewise two loads of a dedicated latency ring's
head/tail.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_async 3 ,
.Xr xtc_exec 3 ,
.Xr xtc_osproc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_preempt_involuntary 3
